WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.500
Token security starts by classifying each token by purpose, authority, issuer, validator, storage location, transport path, and lifetime.

2
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:22.760
A correlation ID, reset token, API key, JWT access token, refresh token, CSRF token, OAuth state value, and webhook secret should not share one vague design.

3
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.260
Write down what each token can do and what must happen if it is copied.

4
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.820
Use cryptographically secure randomness for opaque tokens and one-time values.

5
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:38.120
Store token digests when the application only needs to verify a presented token.

6
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:46.220
Protect access tokens, refresh tokens, API keys, reset links, and session IDs as bearer secrets.

7
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:58.560
Use HTTPS, avoid sensitive tokens in URLs, apply secure cookie attributes when cookies are used, and include CSRF defenses where cookie-based authentication needs them.

8
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.800
JWTs require strict validation before claims are trusted.

9
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:10.780
Check signatures, expected algorithms, issuer, audience, expiration, purpose, and key identity.

10
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.440
Keep claims minimal and avoid secrets in token payloads.

11
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:22.100
Do not treat access tokens, refresh tokens, and ID tokens as interchangeable.

12
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:32.120
A valid authentication token still does not replace object-level authorization, tenant authorization, field-level authorization, or business-rule enforcement.

13
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:56.800
A practical first-week plan is direct: inventory token types, find token logging, review JWT validation, check reset-token storage, confirm cookie flags, remove tokens from URLs, review refresh-token handling, add token revocation paths, and add tests for expired, revoked, wrong-audience, wrong-issuer, reused, and wrong-purpose tokens.

14
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:04.020
Token security becomes reliable when it is part of pull requests, tests, monitoring, and incident response.
